        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Classify Query Scope"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1782834056932"
          },
          "code": "CLASSIFY_QUERY_SCOPE",
          "id": "1782833998068",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1782833998069_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "You are a scope classifier for the Production Planned Quantity Deviation Advisor.\n\nRead the current user question from {{$context.$system.$inputMessage}} and classify its main intent into exactly one label: IN_SCOPE or OUT_OF_SCOPE.\n\nIN_SCOPE means the main user goal is analysis or lookup of planned production quantity deviations, planned quantity variances, or planned percent deviations. Supported analysis dimensions are ingredients or materials, products or outputs, resources, and batches. Organization, plant, date-window, threshold, ranking, and similar filters are in scope only when they support this deviation analysis. A question that explicitly compares planned and execution quantity deviations is also IN_SCOPE.\n\nOUT_OF_SCOPE means the main user goal is outside quantity deviation analysis. This includes approvals or transactions; cost, GL, monetary valuation, or monetary variance impact; supplier, procurement, or contract actions; email, password, or account access; forecasting, S&OP, or plan generation; maintenance or EAM actions; integrations or deployments; general chat, jokes, trivia, biography, or unrelated enterprise questions.\n\nClassify by underlying intent and domain, not by keyword matching. A deviation-related word does not make a request in scope when the actual goal is monetary valuation or another excluded activity. A planning-related word does not make forecasting or plan generation in scope. If a request mixes goals, use its primary requested outcome. If the goal is unclear or does not clearly request supported quantity deviation analysis, classify it as OUT_OF_SCOPE.\n\nExamples are illustrative only; apply the intent rules to paraphrases and unseen wording. Illustrative IN_SCOPE examples include showing top planned ingredient deviations, finding planned product variance above a threshold, and comparing planned versus execution quantity deviation. Illustrative OUT_OF_SCOPE examples include approving a purchase requisition, explaining the monetary amount of variances, generating an S&OP forecast, resetting a password, deploying an integration, or telling a joke.\n\nReturn strict JSON only. Return exactly one of these two objects:\n{\"intent\":\"IN_SCOPE\"}\n{\"intent\":\"OUT_OF_SCOPE\"}\nDo not return markdown, reasoning, confidence, extra fields, or any text outside the JSON object."
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "{\n  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-04/schema#\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"intent\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\",\n      \"enum\": [\n        \"IN_SCOPE\",\n        \"OUT_OF_SCOPE\"\n      ]\n    }\n  },\n  \"required\": [\n    \"intent\"\n  ],\n  \"additionalProperties\": false\n}\n",
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Switch Query Scope"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "IN_SCOPE": "1776582813965",
            "OUT_OF_SCOPE": "1782834122958"
          },
          "code": "SWITCH_QUERY_SCOPE",
          "convergenceTargetId": "end",
          "id": "1782834056932",
          "inputs": [
